# Advice about cost, deliberately kept out of extension.neon.
#
# The rules in extension.neon are terms of the contract - break one and the
# builder is wrong. A rule in here only says a builder is expensive, which is a
# different claim, so a project opts into it on purpose rather than inheriting it
# with the grammar.
#
# The rule ships two entries it can defend as fact and guesses at nothing else.
# Whatever else is expensive in your project is your knowledge, so declare it and
# say why, because the reason ends up in the message a reader has to act on.
#
# Declare only calls whose result is an immutable VALUE. The advice the message
# gives is to hoist or memoise, and that is safe for a string or an int and wrong
# for an object - one instance shared by every builder means a test mutating it
# mutates it for all of them, which is the isolation this package exists to give.
# A nested builder is the clearest example of what NOT to declare. If a valid
# default needs a related entity, building one per seed() is the price of that
# default being valid, not a cost to remove.
#
#   parameters:
#       immutableTestBuilder:
#           costlyInSeed:
#               'App\Security\Hasher::derive': 'its iteration count'
#               'my_slow_helper': 'the sleep it does'
#
# A bare name is a function, a name with :: is a static call. Instance calls are
# not matched, because a builder cannot hold an injected service - the kernel
# seals the constructor - so an instance call in seed() is the builder talking to
# itself.
parameters:
    immutableTestBuilder:
        costlyInSeed: []

parametersSchema:
    immutableTestBuilder: structure([
        costlyInSeed: arrayOf(string(), string())
    ])

services:
    -
        class: Pizgariu\ImmutableTestBuilder\PHPStan\Rule\Performance\CostlyCallInSeedRule
        arguments:
            costlyInSeed: %immutableTestBuilder.costlyInSeed%
        tags:
            - phpstan.rules.rule
